\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Rest, and one spike}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{At #{I_{\mathrm{app}}=0}, let #{F(V)} be the ionic current of
the [Hodgkin–Huxley system](fnip-0071) with #{n}, #{m}, and #{h}
at their steady values for that voltage. For the parameters of
that definition, #{F} is strictly increasing on
#{-90\le V\le 40} and changes sign, so the system has one
[equilibrium](fnip-0014) in the interval.}

\p{\citet{p. 24}{ermentrout2010mathematical} records that
#{\tau_{m}} is considerably smaller than #{\tau_{n}} and
#{\tau_{h}}. At this equilibrium one eigenvalue of the
[Jacobian](fnip-0034) has absolute value of that larger size, and
the other three are smaller. One of those three is real, and the
other two form a complex conjugate pair. Each of the 4 eigenvalues
has negative real part, so the equilibrium is
[hyperbolic](fnip-0034). The signs are computed for these
parameters. The linearization is the one in \ref{fnip-0026}.}

\p{Raise the voltage by #{10} mV from the equilibrium, leave
#{n}, #{m}, and #{h} at their equilibrium values, and take
#{I_{\mathrm{app}}=0}. The solution produces one spike and the
voltage returns to the equilibrium.
\citet{p. 26}{ermentrout2010mathematical} starts Figure 1.14 from
that initial condition: the cell at rest, depolarized by #{10} mV
at time #{0}.}

